<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Konya is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the ancient Iconium. It became the capital of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, the Anatolian branch of the Seljuk world, and flourished as a centre of trade, architecture and learning. It is also associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, who lived and died there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konya.jpg" alt="Konya" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Konya Karatay Belediyesi · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konyaaltincarsi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire straddled the principal Silk Road arteries linking China and Central Asia to the Mediterranean and the Levant, and the sultans drew substantial revenue from caravan traffic through cities such as Nishapur, Rayy, Isfahan and later, in Anatolia, <b>Konya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kilij Arslan rebuilt his state around <b>Konya</b>, which became the permanent capital under his son Mesud I, and in 1101 his army destroyed the crusading expeditions that followed in the wake of the First Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultans of the Seljuks of Anatolia took titles from ancient Persian mythology such as Kai Khosrow and Kay Kāvus, and according to Ibn Bibi, in 1221 Ala' al-Din Kay-kubad decorated the walls of <b>Konya</b> and Sivas with its verses.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seljuk sultan Kilij Arslan II ambushed the army of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos in a narrow pass as it marched on <b>Konya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hamidids ruled the lake district around Egirdir, Isparta, Burdur and Uluborlu, a land of orchards and pastures on the routes between <b>Konya</b> and the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamidids">Hamidids</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1250 they rose against the weakened Sultanate of Rum, and Karaman's son Mehmed Bey briefly occupied <b>Konya</b> in 1277, where he reportedly decreed that Turkish alone be used in government.</blockquote><a href="/state/karamanids">Karamanids</a></li></ul></section>
